  "statement": {
    "case_for": "The recollision model gave strong-field physics a predictive account of how an electron can be driven away from an atom, returned by the field, and emit high-energy radiation. It underpins high-harmonic generation and attosecond methods that resolve electronic dynamics on their natural timescale. Decades of experimental work have tested and extended this picture, making it a mature contribution to fundamental understanding.",
    "case_against": "As Johansson-Goran notes, this is a theoretical framework rather than one sharply bounded experimental event. The citation must distinguish the three-step model from the wider development of high-harmonic generation and attosecond science, and the historical division of credit among its complementary contributors deserves careful review.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson-Goran sees the recollision model as a clear physical account that helped establish attosecond science, while questioning whether the achievement is sufficiently bounded and noting the many later experimental contributions.",
        "response": "I share that concern about scope. The proposed citation keeps the focus on the recollision model and its explanation of high-harmonic generation; later experimental advances show its reach, but need not all be folded into the credited discovery."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll-Stefan argues that optical lattice clocks have achieved extraordinary precision, with Katori and Ye making complementary contributions, while leaving open which fundamental tests or applications will prove most consequential.",
        "response": "The clocks have an exceptionally strong record of reproducibility and precision, and I retain them as an alternative. Their defining scientific impact is still developing, whereas the recollision model has already provided a durable explanation and experimental foundation for observing electron motion in time."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The historical record should clarify how the complementary contributions of Schafer, Lewenstein, and Corkum map onto the specific recollision model.",
      "The boundary between the model itself and the broader development of high-harmonic generation and attosecond science needs careful definition."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
